use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Positioning import Positioning
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence
from ../Dialog import Dialog
from ../Popover import Popover

export ui-component Tour:
    description:
        A tour walks the user through part of an app one step at a time, such
        as a first-run introduction. Each step points out one thing on the page
        with a short explanation, and the user moves forwards and backwards
        through the steps or leaves at any point.

    composes:
        - @{Popover}
        - @{Dialog}

    behaviors:
        - @{Positioning}
        - @{Presence}

    steps:
        A tour is an ordered list of steps. Each step has a title and some
        text, and usually points at one thing on the page. A step can instead
        be a Dialog in the middle of the screen, such as a welcome at the start
        or a summary at the end, or a panel that isn't attached to anything.

    highlight:
        The thing a step points at is picked out, usually by dimming the rest
        of the page around it, and the step's panel sits beside it, attached
        like a Popover. If it is off screen, the page scrolls to it first.
        Everything moves with it if the page scrolls or the layout changes.

    navigation:
        Each step has buttons to go to the next and previous step, and to leave
        the tour. The last step's next button finishes it. The component can
        let Left and Right move between steps too; for right-to-left
        languages, they swap. The component can also jump to any step.

    progress:
        Each step shows where the user is, such as "Step 2 of 5".

    waiting:
        A step can wait for something to happen before the tour moves on, such
        as the user opening a panel, or part of the page loading. Nothing is
        shown while it waits. If the thing a step points at never appears, the
        component decides whether to skip the step or wait.

    page:
        The component decides whether the page can be used during a step. It
        can block the page like a Dialog, or leave the highlighted thing usable
        so the user can try it.

    focus:
        When a step appears, focus moves into it and assistive tools read out
        its title and text. Leaving the tour puts focus back where it was
        before the tour started.

    ending:
        The user can leave with a close button or Escape, and the component can
        let clicking outside do the same. The component is told whether the
        tour was finished, skipped by the user, or closed. That lets it decide
        whether to show the tour again later.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't remember which tours the user has already seen. That's up
            to the app
        - Not for a one-off hint on a single thing. That's a Tooltip or
            Popover
